DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The AALLTTQQ system is a framework to manage queuing disciplines on network
     interfaces.  AALLTTQQ introduces new macros to manipulate output queues.  The
     output queue macros are used to abstract queue operations and not to
     touch the internal fields of the output queue structure.  The macros are
     independent from the AALLTTQQ implementation, and compatible with the tradi‐
     tional _i_f_q_u_e_u_e macros for ease of transition.

     IIFFQQ__EENNQQUUEEUUEE(), IIFFQQ__HHAANNDDOOFFFF() and IIFFQQ__HHAANNDDOOFFFF__AADDJJ() enqueue a packet _m to
     the queue _i_f_q.  The underlying queuing discipline may discard the packet.
     The _e_r_r_o_r argument is set to 0 on success, or ENOBUFS if the packet is
     discarded.  The packet pointed to by _m will be freed by the device driver
     on success, or by the queuing discipline on failure, so the caller should
     not touch _m after enqueuing.  IIFFQQ__HHAANNDDOOFFFF() and IIFFQQ__HHAANNDDOOFFFF__AADDJJ() combine
     the enqueue operation with statistic generation and call iiff__ssttaarrtt() upon
     successful enqueue to initiate the actual send.

     IIFFQQ__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE() dequeues a packet from the queue.  The dequeued packet is
     returned in _m, or _m is set to NULL if no packet is dequeued.  The caller
     must always check _m since a non-empty queue could return NULL under rate-
     limiting.

     IIFFQQ__PPOOLLLL__NNOOLLOOCCKK() returns the next packet without removing it from the
     queue.  The caller must hold the queue mutex when calling
     IIFFQQ__PPOOLLLL__NNOOLLOOCCKK() in order to guarantee that a subsequent call to
     IIFFQQ__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE__NNOOLLOOCCKK() dequeues the same packet.

     IIFFQQ__**__NNOOLLOOCCKK() variants (if available) always assume that the caller
     holds the queue mutex.  They can be grabbed with IIFFQQ__LLOOCCKK() and released
     with IIFFQQ__UUNNLLOOCCKK().

     IIFFQQ__PPUURRGGEE() discards all the packets in the queue.  The purge operation
     is needed since a non-work conserving queue cannot be emptied by a de‐
     queue loop.

     IIFFQQ__IISS__EEMMPPTTYY() can be used to check if the queue is empty.  Note that
     IIFFQQ__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE() could still return NULL if the queuing discipline is non-
     work conserving.

     IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE() moves up to _i_f_q_-_>_i_f_q___d_r_v___m_a_x_l_e_n packets from the queue
     to the “driver managed” queue and returns the first one via _m.  As for
     IIFFQQ__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE(), _m can be NULL even for a non-empty queue.  Subsequent
     calls to IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE() pass the packets from the “driver managed”
     queue without obtaining the queue mutex.  It is the responsibility of the
     caller to protect against concurrent access.  Enabling AALLTTQQ for a given
     queue sets _i_f_q___d_r_v___m_a_x_l_e_n to 0 as the “bulk dequeue” performed by
     IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE() for higher values of _i_f_q___d_r_v___m_a_x_l_e_n is adverse to
     AALLTTQQ's internal timing.  Note that a driver must not mix IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__**()
     macros with the default dequeue macros as the default macros do not look
     at the “driver managed” queue which might lead to an mbuf leak.

     IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__PPRREEPPEENNDD() prepends _m to the “driver managed” queue from where it
     will be obtained with the next call to IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__DDEEQQUUEEUUEE().

     IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__PPUURRGGEE() flushes all packets in the “driver managed” queue and
     calls to IIFFQQ__PPUURRGGEE() afterwards.

     IIFFQQ__DDRRVV__IISS__EEMMPPTTYY() checks for packets in the “driver managed” part of the
     queue.  If it is empty, it forwards to IIFFQQ__IISS__EEMMPPTTYY().

     IIFFQQ__SSEETT__MMAAXXLLEENN() sets the queue length limit to the default FIFO queue.
     The _i_f_q___d_r_v___m_a_x_l_e_n member of the _i_f_a_l_t_q structure controls the length
     limit of the “driver managed” queue.

     IIFFQQ__IINNCC__LLEENN() and IIFFQQ__DDEECC__LLEENN() increment or decrement the current queue
     length in packets.  This is mostly for internal purposes.

     IIFFQQ__IINNCC__DDRROOPPSS() increments the drop counter and is identical to
     IIFF__DDRROOPP().  It is defined for naming consistency only.

     IIFFQQ__SSEETT__RREEAADDYY() sets a flag to indicate that a driver was converted to
     use the new macros.  AALLTTQQ can be enabled only on interfaces with this
     flag.

